##RenderMan RIB
version 3.03

# -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Test 1 diagnostic, LIGHT-SIDE fork: identical to furnace_alum.rib
# except PxrDomeLight -> PxrSphereLight, per the fallback note at the
# bottom of furnace_alum.rib and the open question in
# results-section-draft.md Sec 5.x.3.
#
# PURPOSE: furnace_alum.rib (dome light) showed a mismatch between the
# rendered sphere and the un-rendered rho(theta_o) reference, while its
# OWN background read ~1.0 (dome correctly reaching the camera). That
# does not yet tell you whether the dome's intensity is *also* failing
# to propagate to BSDF-sampled (indirect) rays specifically -- the
# known risk from methodology-and-contributions.md Sec 3.4 -- or
# whether the plugin's own sampler/PDF code (NBrdf.cpp's
# GenerateSample/EvaluateSample) has a bug independent of light type.
#
# PxrSphereLight was NOT observed to have the Sec 3.4 propagation
# problem in prior tests on this project. So:
#   - If THIS render's sphere now matches the compute_albedo.py curve
#     (within noise) -> the furnace_alum.rib mismatch was the dome
#     recurring, i.e. a RenderMan light-propagation issue, NOT a bug
#     in NBrdf.cpp's sampling code. Fix = work around/avoid
#     PxrDomeLight for indirect-light-dependent tests, or escalate to
#     Pixar; the plugin's GenerateSample/EvaluateSample pdf code does
#     not need auditing on this evidence.
#   - If the SAME mismatch persists here -> the dome is cleared as a
#     suspect (two different light types, same symptom), and the
#     defect is in the plugin's own mixture-PDF code. Next step:
#     furnace_alum_cosine.rib / furnace_alum_ggx.rib (samplingMode
#     ablation) to localize it to one specific pdf formula or to the
#     0.5/0.5 combination itself.
#
# Run exactly like furnace_alum.rib's furnace_check.py command -- same
# --screen-half/--res/--sphere-radius, same albedo_alum.csv reference,
# since the camera/sphere geometry is unchanged, only the light block
# differs.
#
# CALIBRATION CAVEAT (carried over from furnace_alum.rib's own fallback
# note): "int areaNormalize" behavior varies by RenderMan version --
# confirm the background still reads ~1.0 below before trusting the
# sphere comparison. If it doesn't, adjust "float intensity" until it
# does, then re-derive what un-rendered rho curve it should be compared
# against (compute_albedo.py's curve assumes unit incoming radiance).
#
# v2 fix (2026-10-14): first run came back with background=0.0, not
# ~1.0 -- the light wasn't illuminating anything. Cause: a sphere light
# emits from its OUTWARD-facing normal by default (like a bulb glowing
# away from its own surface). The camera and test sphere both sit
# INSIDE this radius-50 enclosing sphere, so they were looking at the
# back of the emitting surface, which emits nothing inward. Fixed below
# with `ReverseOrientation` before the Light call, RenderMan's standard
# RIB directive for flipping which side of a surface's normal faces
# "out" -- this flips the sphere's emission to face inward, toward the
# test geometry, without needing a light-specific "sides" parameter
# (and it stays scoped to this AttributeBegin/End block only).
# -----------------------------------------------------------------

Display "furnace_alum_spherelight.exr" "file" "rgba"
Format 512 512 1
PixelFilter "box" 1 1

Projection "orthographic"
ScreenWindow -1.2 1.2 -1.2 1.2
Clipping 0.1 1000
Translate 0 0 5

Hider "raytrace" "int minsamples" [512] "int maxsamples" [512]

WorldBegin

    # Large enclosing sphere light, radius 50, centered at the origin --
    # subtends (nearly) the full hemisphere from every point on the unit
    # test sphere, i.e. the same "uniform white dome" condition as
    # PxrDomeLight, but via a light type not observed to have the Sec 3.4
    # propagation bug. areaNormalize=0 so "intensity" is the quantity to
    # calibrate directly against the background check below, matching
    # the convention furnace_check.py's background check assumes.
    AttributeBegin
        Translate 0 0 0
        ReverseOrientation
        Light "PxrSphereLight" "furnace"
            "float intensity" [1.0]
            "float exposure" [0]
            "color lightColor" [1.0 1.0 1.0]
            "int areaNormalize" [0]
        Scale 50 50 50
    AttributeEnd

    # Same material, same geometry as furnace_alum.rib -- only the light
    # changed, so any change in the sphere's radial profile isolates to
    # the light type.
    Bxdf "NBrdf" "furnace_material"
        "string weightsPath" ["C:/NBrdfBuild/nbrdf_weights.bin"]
    Sphere 1 -1 1 360

WorldEnd
